WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.000
Last year, the New York Times published
the 1619 Project,

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:10.320
an immensely ambitious, influential and&nbsp;
controversial reframing of American history.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:14.880
The project's creator, Nikole Hannah-Jones,&nbsp;
who won a Pulitzer Prize for her work

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:19.680
argued that the U.S. Constitution was&nbsp;
a decidedly undemocratic document,

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:24.320
and that anti-black racism runs&nbsp;
in the very DNA of this country.

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:27.907
Our country says that we were&nbsp;
founded to be a democracy,

00:00:27.907 --> 00:00:30.776
but we were actually founded as a slavocracy.

00:00:30.776 --> 00:00:35.412
This interpretation of the American founding&nbsp;
has been the subject of a heated debate.

00:00:35.412 --> 00:00:40.240
The 1619 Project has also been adapted&nbsp;
into a high school curriculum that attempts

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:43.577
to reframe U.S. history by&nbsp;
marking the year when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.577 --> 00:00:46.720
first enslaved Africans arrived on Virginia soil

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:49.440
as our nation's foundational date.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:54.232
After the 1619 Project was&nbsp;
attacked by five eminent historians,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.232 --> 00:00:57.062
the Projects Editor responded by reiterating

00:00:57.062 --> 00:01:00.000
Hannah-Jones' view, that&nbsp;
advances for minority groups

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.160
have almost always come as result&nbsp;
of political and social struggles

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:07.520
in which African-Americans&nbsp;
have generally taken the lead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:11.520
not as a working-out of the&nbsp;
immanent logic of the Constitution.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:15.280
But this view of the Constituion and&nbsp;
the Declaration of Independence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:17.908
is at odds with that of Frederick Douglass,

00:01:17.908 --> 00:01:23.520
the escaped slave, abolitionist, author,&nbsp;
and towering figure in American history.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:25.840
In his new book A Glorious Liberty:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:29.680
Frederick Douglass and the Fight&nbsp;
for an Antislavery Constitution,

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:33.440
Reason Senior Editor Damon Root looks&nbsp;
at the arguments that took place

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:37.577
in the middle of the 19th century&nbsp;
over racism and America’s founding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.577 --> 00:01:39.728
which are being replayed today.

00:01:39.728 --> 00:01:42.720
If you look at what Frederick Douglass and&nbsp;
William Lloyd Garrison were fighting over

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:48.560
in 1840s and 1850s, you see what people are&nbsp;
fighting about right now, which was the founding.

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:52.597
Just a pro-slavery compact and&nbsp;
Douglass completely rejects that.

00:01:52.597 --> 00:01:57.920
As a former slave, Douglass had no illusions&nbsp;
about racism, but his view of the constitution,

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:02.153
which he called a glorious liberty&nbsp;
document would put him at odds

00:02:02.153 --> 00:02:04.567
with many of its contemporary critics.

00:02:04.567 --> 00:02:08.480
Douglass' challenged really is that the&nbsp;
constitution and the Declaration of Independence

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:11.909
lay out these powerful principles,&nbsp;
but why don't we live up to them?

00:02:11.909 --> 00:02:14.560
Clearly, the nation is not&nbsp;
living up to those principles.

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:17.840
Habeas corpus, due process of law,&nbsp;
the right to keep and bear arms.

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:21.440
Any of those things if sort of&nbsp;
fully enforced, slavery can't stand.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:24.363
And he would point that out&nbsp;
again and again and again.

00:02:24.363 --> 00:02:26.800
Root argues that Douglass was a classical
liberal,

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:30.880
who believed in the primacy of&nbsp;
individual rights and economic freedom.

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:33.280
One of his intellectual adversaries was the&nbsp;

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:37.680
slavery apologist George&nbsp;
Fitzhugh, Who's 1854 book,

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:43.680
Sociology For The South or The Failure of&nbsp;
Free Society, defended slavery by repudiating

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:45.440
the principles of liberalism.

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:49.440
He has this attack on John Locke, attack on Adam&nbsp;
Smith, attack on all these liberal principles,

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:52.960
and you see Frederick Douglass picking up&nbsp;
the banner of those principles and making

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:56.240
it a bedrock of his all-out war on slavery.

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:00.320
He talked about the powerful experience of&nbsp;
earning his first free dollar on the docks

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:03.360
in New Bedford, Massachusetts, when&nbsp;
he escapes and what that meant to him,

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:07.680
that he was now his own master, a tremendous&nbsp;
fact, he writes in one of his autobiographies.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:10.000
It all comes back to this idea of self ownership.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:14.000
In Locke's theory of self ownership, property&nbsp;
rights begins with the ownership in yourself

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:15.600
and everything flows out from that.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:20.000
Unlike many of his contemporaries,&nbsp;
Douglass extended his beliefs in unalienable,

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:22.758
individual rights to women as well.

00:03:22.758 --> 00:03:27.840
In 1848, he attended the Seneca Falls Convention&nbsp;
that launched the women's suffrage movement.

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:29.840
There are 100 people at the&nbsp;
Seneca falls convention.

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:32.320
He's one of 32 men, he's the only black person
there.

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:36.080
He takes the radical position&nbsp;
alongside Elizabeth Cady Stanton,

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:41.040
that women should have—not just a whole host of other legal privileges that they're denied at the time—

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:42.757
but women should have the right to vote.

00:03:42.757 --> 00:03:46.582
Stanton said that was almost&nbsp;too radical for the convention, there were people who were against it.

00:03:46.582 --> 00:03:48.401
They thought, "Oh, we can't go that far," and

00:03:48.401 --> 00:03:51.203
Douglass was right there, shoulder&nbsp;
to shoulder with her on that issue.

00:03:51.203 --> 00:03:55.440
Douglass would have a bitter falling out with&nbsp;
Elizabeth Cady Stanton and other suffragettes

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:59.797
after the Civil War, when only former&nbsp;
male slaves got the right to vote,

00:03:59.797 --> 00:04:03.000
but he would reconcile with&nbsp;
them later in his life.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:07.520
The last thing that Douglass does the day he&nbsp;
dies is he attends a womens rights meeting,

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:11.788
that's his last public act and he goes home&nbsp;
and dies of either a heart attack or a stroke.

00:04:11.788 --> 00:04:13.428
He falls dead in his home.

00:04:13.428 --> 00:04:17.782
So he goes out doing what he always&nbsp;
did, fighting for liberty and equality.

00:04:17.782 --> 00:04:22.789
In a country that seems more divided along&nbsp;
racial and ethnic lines than it has been in decades,

00:04:22.789 --> 00:04:26.160
Root says that Frederick Douglass'&nbsp;
belief in principles undergirding

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:30.320
the American founding have a lot to&nbsp;
teach us about how to build a society

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:34.080
based on mutual respect,&nbsp;
empathy, and individualism.

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:37.920
Douglass, he says, stressed that&nbsp;
rights for one are rights for all,

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:41.252
ending slavery was also something&nbsp;
that benefited the country as a whole,

00:04:41.252 --> 00:04:42.960
because slavery is a pernicious institution,

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:45.828
it's economically crippling,
all these sorts of things.

00:04:45.828 --> 00:04:47.908
A free labor system benefits whites and blacks,&nbsp;

00:04:47.908 --> 00:04:50.872
to the extent that a civil rights cause looks like it just benefits one group,

00:04:50.872 --> 00:04:54.437
in fact, the benefits are&nbsp;
far and wide to all involved.

00:04:54.437 --> 00:04:57.360
That's certainly a lesson we&nbsp;
can continue to take today.

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:03.200
The nation's founding doesn't need to be dialed&nbsp;
back to 1619 to acknowledge the horrific legacy

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:08.532
of slavery and honor the major role played&nbsp;
by African-Americans in the nation's history

00:05:08.532 --> 00:05:13.032
we would do well not only to remember the&nbsp;
example set by Frederick Douglass in his life,

00:05:13.032 --> 00:05:18.502
but also to embrace his understanding of the&nbsp;
Constitution as a glorious liberty document,

00:05:18.502 --> 00:05:23.309
whose ideals of freedom and equality&nbsp;
we must never stop fighting to realize.

